Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||02-02-2000||12:20 AM||||let me clarify..<P>I'm looking for source code (preferably) that will allow a windows application to upload files to a remote server, the way PIP does.<P>I'm not referring to an FTP client, the code is for some software being written that acts as a backend for a database. The people that will be using it are largely computer illiterate, so that's why I want a single program that will allow them to enter data, upload files, delete files, etc.<BR>Otherwise I'd tell them to use an FTP program.. <P>I had to teach them how to save files to a floppy disk.. their "my documents" folder has tons of files in it, with every document they've saved in the past year or so... they didn't know how to save anywhere else.<p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 02-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||02-02-2000||12:53 AM||||you can teach them to save things with the "send to" when you right mouse click items<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||02-02-2000||04:27 AM||||Sending files using FTP or HTTP is not easy... You'll have to work around the incredible amount of bugs in the WinAPI. For something like this it's probably the easiest to use Windows Explorer (you'll need to have IE 5.0 installed). You can create a shortcut on your desktop to an explorer window that will automatically open an ftp server, displaying it as it would display an ordinary harddisk. To test this, just open an explorer window and in the address field, type<P><B>ftp://yourusername:yourpassword@ftp.yourserver.com</B><P>You'll notice that the ftp server will be opened as a location and will appear in the left column. You can then simply drag files to copy them to the server as you would copy to an ordinary harddisk.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||02-02-2000||04:31 AM||||PS: Please notice that I'm talking about <B>Windows</B> Explorer, not <B>Internet</B> Explorer.||||reg||1
